import sys
from pathlib import Path
TD=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;sys.path.insert(0,str(TD.parent))
from manuscript_support import build
E=[
('01-00','Density and distribution','Population density measures people per unit area; distribution describes where people live and how they are spread.','A mean density can conceal concentrations. Density equals population divided by area, using comparable dates and boundaries.'),
('01-01','Population density','Population density is the number of people per unit area, commonly people per square kilometre.','A fictional 12,000 residents over 300 km² gives 12,000 ÷ 300 = 40 people per km²; it does not mean each square kilometre contains 40 people.'),
('01-02','Population distribution','Population distribution is the spatial pattern showing where people live and how they are spread across an area.','Describe clusters, sparse areas and relationships to locations using map evidence. A number per square kilometre does not describe the full spatial pattern.'),
('02-00','Explain settlement patterns','Climate, relief, economic opportunities, services and political conditions can influence settlement.','Southern African settlement is uneven; water and suitable terrain can support concentration, while jobs and infrastructure may attract people even where physical conditions are difficult. One factor seldom explains the whole pattern.'),
('02-01','Climate and relief','Water availability, temperature and terrain affect the feasibility of settlement and livelihoods.','Gentler accessible land with usable water may support farming and building more readily than a dry steep area; infrastructure can modify some constraints.'),
('02-02','Economic factors','Jobs, markets and transport can draw people towards productive opportunities.','A viable workplace near a usable route can encourage settlement; a mineral deposit without access or work opportunities need not attract a large population.'),
('02-03','Social factors','Schools, health facilities, family networks and other services can influence where people live.','A service concentration may improve access, but housing and travel costs affect whether households can use it. Availability is different from equal access.'),
('02-04','Political factors','Safety, displacement, boundaries and public settlement decisions can affect population distribution.','Conflict can force movement regardless of a place’s productive potential; safer conditions and usable rights can support return. Do not assume movements are freely chosen.'),
('03-00','Growth and rates','Population changes through births, deaths and net migration; comparable rates allow populations of different sizes to be assessed.','Natural increase excludes migration. Higher population level differs from faster increase: compare differences or rates over equal intervals, not the largest final value alone.'),
('03-01','Growth rate','Population growth rate measures change in population over a period relative to its starting population, expressed as a percentage for that period.','Use total change including migration where appropriate. A growth rate can fall while population still rises if the rate remains positive.'),
('03-02','Crude birth rate','Crude birth rate is live births during a year per thousand people in the population.','Use births divided by the relevant population, multiplied by 1,000. It differs from births per woman and from a total number of births.'),
('03-03','Death rate','The crude death rate measures deaths during a year per thousand people in the population.','A crude rate is influenced by age structure as well as health conditions, so a higher rate does not by itself prove worse services.'),
('03-04','Infant mortality','Infant mortality rate is deaths before the first birthday per thousand live births, for a stated period.','Its denominator is live births, not total population; compare definitions and periods before interpreting a difference.'),
('03-05','Demographic transition model','The demographic transition model simplifies changes in birth and death rates and consequent natural population growth during development.','The broad four-stage account moves from high birth/death rates, through falling deaths then falling births, towards low rates. It is a model, not an identical timetable for every country; migration is not its central mechanism.'),
('04-00','Population policy','Policies may seek slower or faster growth through services, incentives or restrictions, but need evaluation against well-being and rights.','A numerical target alone does not establish a beneficial outcome. Age structure, choices, implementation and long-term effects matter; historical examples must not be presented as current law.'),
('04-01','Reducing growth: a historical example','China’s historical one-child policy illustrates restrictions intended to reduce births; UNFPA recorded the move towards a two-child policy in 2015.','Restrictions can affect births but create rights and family-welfare concerns. Evaluate voluntary information and services alongside any growth aim rather than endorsing coercion or assuming policy alone explains fertility.'),
('04-02','Increasing growth: a historical example','Singapore moved in 1987 from its earlier two-child approach towards encouraging larger families, as recorded by its National Library.','Support with childcare, housing or parenting costs can address some barriers, but preferences, work and wider conditions mean an incentive need not produce the intended birth increase.'),
('05-00','Use population data','Population data can guide the location and capacity of services, employment planning and infrastructure.','For a Namibian planning application, compare district age groups and access to schools or clinics rather than distributing capacity solely by national totals. Use the date and local denominators.'),
('05-01','Economic uses','Age and location data can inform workforce training, transport demand and markets.','Counts of working-age residents do not directly measure available skills or job seekers; combine population structure with relevant labour information.'),
('05-02','Social uses','Children’s and older residents’ counts can inform school, health and care planning.','Needs depend on location and actual access as well as age. A distant facility may offer less usable capacity than its place count suggests.'),
('06-00','Population pyramids','A population pyramid shows age and sex composition, with age groups vertically and horizontal bars for each sex on a consistent count or percentage scale.','A broad base indicates a relatively large young group; a narrower base and substantial older groups suggest a different age structure. Shape alone does not prove causes or future certainty.'),
('06-01','Older age structures','Many developed economies have relatively narrower young bases and larger older shares than youthful populations.','These tendencies can inform care and workforce questions but do not describe every country or person. Compare the same age bands and units.'),
('06-02','Youthful age structures','A youthful pyramid has a relatively broad young base compared with older age groups.','A large school-age share can imply future education demand, while future births also depend on survival, migration and fertility choices; do not infer inevitable rapid growth from shape alone.'),
('07-00','HIV, AIDS and population','HIV is a virus affecting the immune system; AIDS is an advanced stage of untreated HIV infection. Effective treatment and accessible support change health outcomes.','Illness and premature deaths can affect household care, age patterns and livelihoods, but do not assume HIV means immediate death or inability to work. Health and population judgements need evidence and non-stigmatising language.')]
L={s:(m,e) for s,h,m,e in E};Q={}
def q(n,p,a,g):Q[n]=(p,a,g)
q(1,'Differentiate between population density and distribution.','Density is people per unit area; distribution is the spatial pattern of where people live.',['Comparison: numerical area average versus spatial arrangement.'])
q(2,'Calculate density for a fictional area with 12,000 people and 300 km². Show working.','12,000 ÷ 300 = 40 people per km².',['Working: population divided by area.','Unit: people per km².'])
q(33,'Explain why “people cluster by a river” is not a population-density figure.','It describes distribution. Density needs people divided by area; test whether the statement gives a spatial pattern or people per unit area.',['Error: spatial pattern treated as an area average.','Distinction: distribution versus density.'])
for n,s in [(3,'01-01'),(4,'01-02'),(13,'03-01'),(14,'03-02'),(15,'03-03'),(16,'03-04'),(17,'03-05')]:q(n,'Define '+dict((x[0],x[1]) for x in E)[s].lower()+'.',L[s][0],['Meaning: '+L[s][0]])
q(5,'Explain why jobs can influence population distribution even in a physically difficult location.','Usable work and transport can attract people and support earnings despite natural constraints. The opportunity must be accessible; the physical environment alone does not settle the pattern.',['Reason: link economic opportunity and settlement.'])
q(6,'Describe this fictional map specification: eight of ten settlements lie within 5 km of a river and all three market centres are on its road; the dry western area has two small settlements.','Settlement is concentrated along the river-road corridor while the dry west is sparse. The settlement and market observations associate water and access with concentration without isolating either as the sole cause.',['Evidence: use both spatial observations.','Relationship: infer corridor concentration rather than merely listing places.'])
cases={7:('a flat river valley has usable water while nearby steep dry land has few settlements','Water and accessible terrain support settlement and production in the valley; the contrast makes the physical explanation plausible.'),8:('an accessible mine offers jobs and new housing, while an unworked deposit has neither','The accessible employment and housing opportunity can attract residents; the deposit alone does not supply a livelihood.'),9:('households move near a school with places while distant journeys are costly','Usable education access reduces a service barrier and can influence residential choice, subject to housing affordability.'),10:('families leave a productive area because conflict makes residence unsafe','Security overrides productive opportunity: forced departure can reduce settlement despite useful resources.')}
for n,(case,a) in cases.items():q(n,'Explain the settlement mechanism in this fictional case: '+case+'.',a,['Mechanism: use both supplied conditions.','Consequence: '+a])
q(11,'Calculate natural increase per thousand where a fictional population has birth rate 32 and death rate 12 per thousand in the same year.','32 - 12 = 20 per thousand per year.',['Working: birth rate minus death rate.','Unit: per thousand per year; migration excluded.'])
q(12,'Describe this fictional series over equal intervals: A grows from 100 to 120 thousand; B from 300 to 310 thousand.','B remains larger, but A increases by 20 thousand versus B’s 10 thousand. Level and absolute increase therefore give different comparisons.',['Evidence: compare levels and changes.','Relationship: distinguish size from increase; no rate calculation is compulsory.'])
q(32,'Explain why choosing the final population of 310 thousand does not identify the largest increase when another place rises from 100 to 120 thousand and this place from 300 to 310 thousand.','The largest level is 310, but the increases are 20 and 10 thousand respectively. Test change across the interval rather than selecting the highest final value.',['Error: highest level substitutes for largest increase.','Distinction: level versus difference.'])
q(18,'Explain which policy better meets a fictional aim of slower growth with well-being: voluntary accessible information and services, or birth restrictions that deny families necessary support.','Voluntary informed services better fit well-being and rights. Restrictions may affect births but withholding essential support creates harm; a lower growth figure alone cannot justify that trade-off.',['Judgement: compare against growth with well-being.','Reason: weigh intended rate effects and the supplied welfare consequences.'])
q(19,'Suggest a planning response where a fictional district has many young children and too few primary places, while funding can expand local provision.','Use the age and location data to expand accessible primary places and staff them. This addresses present education demand rather than assuming a population target supplies services.',['Context: child numbers, missing places and available funds.','Reason: match capacity to actual need.'])
q(20,'Explain why a historical restrictive birth policy cannot be evaluated from fewer births alone when the evidence also records pressure on family choice.','Fewer births may fit its numerical aim, but constrained choice is a material welfare and rights cost. Compare voluntary alternatives and longer-term age effects; do not attribute the entire fertility trend to one policy.',['Judgement: bounded evaluation against well-being.','Reason: weigh rate aim with choice and alternatives.'])
q(21,'Explain whether a fictional pronatalist childcare subsidy is sufficient when parents report both childcare cost and inflexible work hours, while the alternative also offers usable work-time support.','The subsidy addresses cost but leaves the second barrier. Combined usable support better matches both stated constraints, though it cannot determine private fertility choices or guarantee more births.',['Judgement: compare sufficiency against the stated barriers.','Reason: weigh childcare cost with work-time access.'])
q(22,'Suggest a population-data use for planning fictional Namibian district clinics where older residents are concentrated far from existing facilities.','Combine age/location counts with travel and service data to plan accessible clinic or outreach capacity near the underserved population.',['Context: older residents and distance.','Reason: connect measured need to usable provision.'])
q(23,'Describe this fictional planning comparison: A has 400 school-age children and 200 places; B has 200 children and 200 places.','A has a 200-place shortfall while B’s recorded capacity matches its count. Equal place allocation therefore corresponds to unequal coverage.',['Evidence: use child and place counts in both areas.','Relationship: connect capacity to different denominators.'])
for n,s in [(24,'05-01'),(25,'05-02'),(28,'06-01'),(29,'06-02')]:q(n,'Describe two relevant aspects of '+dict((x[0],x[1]) for x in E)[s].lower()+'.',' '.join(L[s]),['Feature: '+L[s][0],'Feature: '+L[s][1]])
q(26,'Describe the population-pyramid pattern represented by this original age-sex table: A, 0–14 male 20% female 20%, 15–64 male 27% female 27%, 65+ male 3% female 3%; B, 0–14 male 10% female 10%, 15–64 male 30% female 30%, 65+ male 10% female 10%.','A has the broader young base, 40% versus 20%, while B has the larger older share, 20% versus 6%. The age structures suggest different education and care demands, without proving causes.',['Evidence: compare corresponding age groups across both pyramids.','Relationship: infer youthful versus older structure; bars use the same percentage scale.'])
q(27,'Calculate the 0–14 share where an original fictional age-sex table gives 180 boys and 220 girls in that group out of 1,000 total residents.','180 + 220 = 400; 400 ÷ 1,000 = 0.4; multiplying by 100 gives 40%.',['Working: add sexes, divide by total and convert.','Unit: percent of total residents.'])
q(30,'Explain which response better supports a fictional population affected by HIV-related illness: accessible continued treatment and household support, or excluding affected workers despite their capacity to work.','Accessible treatment and support better fit health and livelihood aims. Exclusion removes opportunities without assessing actual capacity and can intensify household costs; HIV status does not establish inability to contribute.',['Judgement: compare health and livelihood outcomes.','Reason: weigh access/support against exclusion, without clinical advice.'])
q(31,'Describe this fictional service series: access to ongoing HIV care rises from 50% to 80% of an eligible group while recorded missed-work days fall from 200 to 120 under the stated definitions.','Wider care access accompanies 80 fewer missed days and a 30-percentage-point coverage gain. The association supports investigating access and participation, but does not establish treatment caused the whole work change.',['Evidence: accurate paired changes.','Relationship: association, with causal limits.'])
P={'P01':(['01-00','05-00'],'State (a) the meanings of density and distribution [2]. Explain (b) why two fictional districts with the same mean density need different clinic locations when one has a single settlement cluster and the other dispersed villages [4].','(a) People per unit area versus spatial arrangement. (b) The equal average hides different access patterns: a central site may serve a cluster while dispersed residents need distributed or outreach provision. Distribution and travel evidence are needed alongside density.',['Point: paired meanings [2].','Development: connect equal average and contrasting locations to usable service provision [4].']),
'P02':(['01-00','05-00'],'Calculate (a) density for fictional A with 9,000 people in 300 km² [2]. Explain (b) whether that figure alone justifies one central school when 70% of children live in dispersed distant villages and no transport is available [4].','(a) 9,000 ÷ 300 = 30 people per km². (b) No: the mean does not show the children’s dispersed locations, and absent transport restricts central access. Investigate local or transport options rather than allocate solely by density.',['Evidence: method and people per km² [2].','Meaning: use distribution and transport to justify the bounded access decision [4].'])}
examples=[(n,'Apply the measure',Q[k][0]+' '+Q[k][1],[s for s in L if s.startswith(n+'-')]) for n,k in [('01',2),('02',6),('03',12),('04',18),('05',23),('06',26),('07',31)]]
examples += [('04','Keep policy examples dated','[UNFPA’s 2015 statement](https://asiapacific.unfpa.org/en/news/statement-chinas-population-policy-changes) records China moving away from its earlier restriction. [Singapore’s National Library](https://www.nlb.gov.sg/main/article-detail?cmsuuid=1d106f7e-aca1-4c0e-ac7a-d35d0772707d) dates its pronatalist change to 1987. These illustrate differing policy aims; neither describes current rules. Checked 20 October 2026.',['04-00','04-01','04-02']),('06','Read and construct the shape','For the fictional table above, place age groups vertically, youngest at the bottom, and percentage bars horizontally, male left and female right. Use equal scales, a sex key, units and a title. A’s base bars extend 20% each side and older bars 3%; B’s are 10% and 10%. The age bands here are broad summaries; use finer equal bands for detailed age comparisons. No unseen plotted image is claimed to have been verified.',['06-00','06-01','06-02']),('07','Health evidence','[WHO’s HIV fact sheet](https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/hiv-aids), checked 20 October 2026, distinguishes HIV from AIDS and explains the role of effective treatment. Ordinary social contact does not transmit HIV. This is conceptual health education, not an individual diagnosis or treatment instruction.',['07-00'])]
links={n:'Connect population evidence to Health (7.2), Education (7.3) and Urbanisation (4.2). Check dates, boundaries, denominators and units before explaining what a pattern supports.' for n in ['01','02','03','04','05','06','07']}
